WEBVTT

00:00.190 --> 00:01.770
- [Instructor] Let's say
someone walks up to you

00:01.770 --> 00:03.710
on the street, and they
give you this expression.

00:03.710 --> 00:08.710
X squared plus 7x plus
10 divided by x plus two.

00:10.300 --> 00:13.550
And they say, see if you
can simplify this thing.

00:13.550 --> 00:16.610
And so, pause this video
and see if you can do that.

00:16.610 --> 00:17.750
And one way to think about it is,

00:17.750 --> 00:22.450
what is x squared plus 7x
plus 10 divided by x plus two,

00:22.450 --> 00:23.700
what is that going to be?

00:24.860 --> 00:26.240
All right now, there's two ways

00:26.240 --> 00:27.330
that you could approach this.

00:27.330 --> 00:29.800
One way is to try to factor the numerator

00:29.800 --> 00:31.770
and see if it has a factor

00:31.770 --> 00:34.390
that is common to the denominator.

00:34.390 --> 00:35.700
So let's try to do that.

00:35.700 --> 00:37.540
So we've done this many, many times.

00:37.540 --> 00:39.110
If this looks new to you,

00:39.110 --> 00:41.100
I encourage you to review
factoring polynomials

00:41.100 --> 00:42.950
other places on Kahn Academy.

00:42.950 --> 00:44.900
But what two numbers add up to seven,

00:44.900 --> 00:47.180
and when you multiply em, you get 10?

00:47.180 --> 00:48.790
Well, that would be two and five.

00:48.790 --> 00:50.340
So we could rewrite that numerator

00:50.340 --> 00:54.600
as x plus two times x plus five.

00:54.600 --> 00:55.433
And then of course,

00:55.433 --> 00:59.090
the denominator, you
still have x plus two.

00:59.090 --> 01:01.800
And then we clearly see
we have a common factor.

01:01.800 --> 01:05.650
And so as long as x does
not equal negative two,

01:05.650 --> 01:07.310
because if x equals negative two,

01:07.310 --> 01:09.170
this whole expression is undefined,

01:09.170 --> 01:11.140
because then you get a
zero in the denominator.

01:11.140 --> 01:15.430
So as long as x does
not equal negative two,

01:15.430 --> 01:17.390
well then, we can divide the numerator

01:17.390 --> 01:19.840
and the denominator by x plus two.

01:19.840 --> 01:21.053
Once again, the reason why
I put that constraint is

01:21.053 --> 01:22.930
that we can't divide the
numerator and the denominator

01:22.930 --> 01:23.920
by zero.

01:23.920 --> 01:25.920
So for any other values of x,

01:25.920 --> 01:28.420
this x plus two will be non zero,

01:28.420 --> 01:29.510
and we could divide the numerator

01:29.510 --> 01:32.180
and the denominator by that,
and they would cancel out,

01:32.180 --> 01:36.540
and we would just be
left with x plus five.

01:36.540 --> 01:39.000
So another way to think
about it is this expression,

01:39.000 --> 01:41.170
our original expression,
could be viewed as

01:41.170 --> 01:46.170
x plus five for any x that
is not equal to negative two.

01:47.430 --> 01:50.060
Now the other way that
we could approach this

01:50.060 --> 01:53.310
is through algebraic long division,

01:53.310 --> 01:55.640
which is very analogous to
the type of long division

01:55.640 --> 01:56.880
that you might remember from,

01:56.880 --> 01:59.060
I believe it was, fourth grade.

01:59.060 --> 02:00.380
So what you do is you say, all right,

02:00.380 --> 02:02.390
I'm gonna divide x plus two

02:02.390 --> 02:07.390
into x squared plus seven x plus 10.

02:07.830 --> 02:10.790
And in this technique, you look
at the highest degree terms.

02:10.790 --> 02:13.340
So then, you have an x there
and an x squared there.

02:13.340 --> 02:16.310
And say, how many times
does x go into x squared?

02:16.310 --> 02:18.120
Well it goes x times.

02:18.120 --> 02:20.070
Now you'd write that in this column,

02:20.070 --> 02:22.830
because x is just x to the first power.

02:22.830 --> 02:24.950
You could view this as
the first degree column.

02:24.950 --> 02:27.890
It's analogous to the place values

02:27.890 --> 02:30.570
that we talk about when
we first learn numbers,

02:30.570 --> 02:33.790
or how we group or about place value.

02:33.790 --> 02:35.720
But here you could view
it as degree places

02:35.720 --> 02:36.740
or something like that.

02:36.740 --> 02:37.960
And then you take that x

02:37.960 --> 02:40.360
and you multiply it times
this entire expression.

02:40.360 --> 02:43.020
So x times two is 2x.

02:43.020 --> 02:44.860
Put that in the first degree column.

02:44.860 --> 02:48.440
X times x is x squared.

02:48.440 --> 02:49.650
And then what we wanna do

02:49.650 --> 02:51.930
is we wanna subtract
these things in yellow

02:51.930 --> 02:54.310
from what we originally had in blue.

02:54.310 --> 02:55.960
So we could do it this way.

02:55.960 --> 03:00.620
And then we will be left
with 7x minus 2x is 5x.

03:00.620 --> 03:03.930
And then x squared minus
x squared is just a zero.

03:03.930 --> 03:08.330
And then we can bring down this plus 10.

03:08.330 --> 03:11.080
And once again, we look at
the highest degree term.

03:11.080 --> 03:13.730
X goes into 5x five times.

03:13.730 --> 03:14.660
That's a zero degree.

03:14.660 --> 03:17.750
It's a constant, so I'll write
it in the constant column.

03:17.750 --> 03:19.990
Five times two is 10.

03:19.990 --> 03:22.430
Five times x is five.

03:22.430 --> 03:25.520
And then I'll subtract these
from what we have up here.

03:25.520 --> 03:27.530
And notice, we have no remainder.

03:27.530 --> 03:29.240
And what's interesting about
algebraic long division,

03:29.240 --> 03:31.180
and we'll probably see it
in another video or two,

03:31.180 --> 03:33.270
you can actually have a remainder.

03:33.270 --> 03:34.850
So those are going to be situations where

03:34.850 --> 03:37.520
just a factoring technique
alone would not have worked.

03:37.520 --> 03:42.170
In this situation, this
model would have been easier,

03:42.170 --> 03:43.500
but this is another way to think about it.

03:43.500 --> 03:44.430
You can say, hey, look,

03:44.430 --> 03:47.860
x plus two times x plus five
is going to be equal to this.

03:47.860 --> 03:50.730
Now, if you wanted to
rewrite this expression

03:50.730 --> 03:52.410
the way we did here, and say, hey,

03:52.410 --> 03:55.000
this expression is equal to x plus five,

03:55.000 --> 03:57.230
we would have to constrain the domain.

03:57.230 --> 04:00.320
You'd say, hey, for all x's
not equaling negative two

04:00.320 --> 04:03.353
for these to be completely
identical expressions.
